# Sketch preprocessor specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies mm.ino and the sketch tool front end. It defines
how .ino sketch files are transformed into standard ISO C++20 application
translation units. It describes implemented behavior, first released in
v1.3.0.

docs/modules-sketch.mdy specifies the runtime environment and vocabulary provided by mm.sketch; docs/modules-c++20.mdy specifies language rules and include permissions in ordinary translation units; docs/modules-mm.mdy specifies the manifest schema, including mm: 1.3 and the sketch: key.

# Role

Sketches rely on toolchain preprocessing: standard headers and prototypes
are inferred, and a main function is provided by the core library.

mm.ino provides this transformation within modules.cpp. It generates a standard C++20 translation unit (main.cpp) containing the necessary module imports, hoisted includes, forward declarations, sketch bodies, and the main entry point that invokes mm::sketch::run.

The transformation is implemented as a pure function in the mm.ino module and exposed via the tools/sketch command-line tool.

# Discovery and manifest conventions

The sketch tool processes an application directory according to two discovery rules:

## Directory with an existing manifest

When the target directory contains mm.mdy:
1. The manifest is authoritative. The sketch files to transform are taken from its sketch: keys, in declared order.
2. Every .ino file present in the directory must be listed in the manifest. If an unlisted .ino file is found, the tool halts with an error naming the unexpected file. This prevents sketches from being silently excluded from compilation.

## Directory without a manifest

When the target directory contains no mm.mdy:
1. The tool searches for a primary sketch whose filename matches the directory name: <directory>/<directory>.ino (for example, apps/ino/blink/blink.ino).
2. If this file is absent, or if additional .ino files exist in the directory, the tool reports an error explaining the naming convention.
3. The tool generates an initial manifest (mm.mdy) specifying mm: 1.3, kind: app, name set to the directory basename, use: mm.sketch, and sketch: naming the single sketch file.
4. Subsequent invocations follow the manifest-driven rule above.

## Parent registration and tree membership

The sketch tool evaluates parent registration across four distinct cases:

- Case 1: External root sketch with project:.
  The directory's own manifest declares project: (pointing to an enclosing
  project). No parent registration check is performed; regeneration and
  --check succeed without requiring an immediate parent.
- Case 2: Sketch within a tree.
  The directory is inside a project tree or nested under an external root.
  The immediate parent manifest is checked for a folder: entry naming the
  sketch directory. On generation, if the folder entry is missing, sketch emits
  a reminder on its final line (such as apps/ino/mm.mdy does not name blink; add
  folder: blink) and exits 0. Under sketch --check, missing parent registration
  fails and exits 65.
- Case 3: Generating outside any tree.
  The target directory has no manifest and lies outside any tree. The tool
  determines the project root via --project DIR or by discovering the project
  installation containing the sketch tool itself. It generates mm.mdy with
  project: <relpath> and outputs a build hint on its final line:
  external sketch; build with <project>/out/bin/build <directory>.
- Case 4: Orphan external sketch.
  The directory lies outside any tree, contains an mm.mdy manifest, but lacks
  a project: key and has no parent registering it. Running sketch on
  generation or with --check refuses the orphan and exits 65:
  sketch: <manifest>: sketch is neither registered by a parent nor
  external; add project: to it or generate in a new directory.

If a manifest attempts both shapes (declaring project: while also being
named by a parent's folder: declaration), sketch --check refuses it.

# Transformation steps

mm.ino transforms the input sketch files into a single main.cpp translation unit across four stages:

## 1. Prelude and hoisted includes

Ordinary translation units in modules.cpp may include standard library headers at the top of the file before any import declarations.

The last line of that region is the compatibility header, which main.cpp includes as #include "Sketch.h". A sketch toolchain supplies that header to every sketch without being asked, so a sketch writes uint8_t, A0, F() and PROGMEM without naming where they come from, and mm.sketch does not export those spellings by itself. It is emitted below the hoisted includes rather than above them because it imports mm.sketch, and a standard library header included after an import declaration is the arrangement the hoist exists to avoid. The legacy profile emits it above them instead, as The legacy profile below describes. The synthesised compatibility header below describes the file itself.

mm.ino scans the sketch text for include directives of either form, #include <...> and #include "...". Every directive found is hoisted to the very top of main.cpp in the order the sketches introduce it, spelled exactly as the sketch wrote it: an angled include stays angled and a quoted include stays quoted. A directive repeated across sketch files is hoisted once. When an include directive is hoisted, an empty placeholder line is retained in the sketch body so that subsequent line positions within that file are preserved.

Quoted includes are how a sketch reaches the header of the library it exercises, which is the ordinary shape of a library's own example sketches. The preprocessor resolves nothing: it copies the directive, and the header is found, or not found, by the include path the build gives the translation unit. A sketch application that includes a header outside its own directory therefore declares the library providing it, as Library sketches below describes.

Following any hoisted standard includes, the tool emits the module prelude:

Opening fence
import mm.sketch;

using namespace mm::sketch;
Closing fence

## 2. Function prototypes

To allow functions in the sketch to be referenced before their definitions, the preprocessor generates forward declarations for eligible function definitions.

Because full C++ parsing is avoided, function definitions are recognized using a restricted syntax heuristic:
- The line must begin at column zero (no leading whitespace).
- The line must occur at brace depth zero (outside any enclosing class, struct, or namespace).
- The signature must match type name(parameters), followed by an opening brace { on the same line or the immediately following line.
- The return type must consist of identifiers with optional qualifiers (*, &, const, ::).

Brace depth is tracked accurately outside string literals, character literals, and comments (both single-line // and block /* ... */).

A generated prototype carries each parameter without its default argument. A default belongs to the one declaration that gives it, and the sketch's definition gives it, so a prototype repeating it would make C++ refuse the definition as a redefinition. The default is dropped from its top-level = to the next top-level comma or the closing parenthesis, so one that is a call, a braced list, a template argument list, or a string or character literal containing a comma goes whole. A call placed above the definition therefore passes every argument, which is also what compiles when the sketch declares the function itself without defaults. The definition line is still matched by counting parentheses without regard to literals, so a default string containing a closing parenthesis ends the parameter list early; such a definition needs its own declaration.

Definitions that span multiple lines before the function name, template functions, and operator overloads are not matched by the heuristic. Sketches utilizing these constructs must provide their own explicit forward declarations; the preprocessor preserves existing declarations without duplicating them. If an explicit declaration is seen after a definition, the generated prototype at the top of the file is kept so earlier callers retain their forward declaration.

If a line at brace depth zero resembles a function definition but begins with leading whitespace, the preprocessor emits a diagnostic warning:

Opening fence
definition not at column zero; no prototype generated; indent it to column zero or declare it
Closing fence

Compilation succeeds if the indented helper is defined prior to its first use.

## 3. Sketch source and line displacement comments

The sketch files are appended in the order specified by the manifest.

Prior to each file's content, the tool writes a comment documenting the source filename and the line displacement introduced by the generated prelude and prototypes:

Opening fence
// blink.ino (displaced by 7 lines)
Closing fence

This enables line numbers reported in compiler diagnostics to be converted back to the original .ino file locations by subtraction.

## 4. Main function entry

The generated translation unit concludes with the standard application entry point:

Opening fence
int main() { return mm::sketch::run(&setup, &loop); }
Closing fence

If a sketch defines its own main function, the preprocessor reports an error identifying the line number.

# Committed output and integrity checks

The generated main.cpp begins with a header identifying the tool version and source files:

Opening fence
// Generated by sketch (mm: 1.3) from blink.ino -- do not edit by hand.
Closing fence

main.cpp is committed to version control beside its source sketch, allowing code inspection without intermediate generation steps. If main.cpp is absent or modified sketch sources require regeneration, tools/build invokes sketch automatically.

Every sketch application also commits six generated headers beside main.cpp: Sketch.h, the compatibility header described under The synthesised compatibility header below, and the five one-line forwarders to it described under The headers a library includes by other names, Arduino.h, Print.h, Printable.h, Wire.h, and SPI.h. main.cpp includes Sketch.h whether or not a library is reached, and a vendored library includes a forwarder by its foreign name, so all six are part of the application's source and none is ignored by version control. A clone therefore checks and builds without running sketch first. All seven generated files are verified by the check below.

The integrity of generated files is verified via:

Opening fence
./sketch --check apps/ino/blink/
Closing fence

The check mode regenerates the content in memory, compares it byte-for-byte with main.cpp, Sketch.h, and each forwarder on disk, and verifies parent registration in mm.mdy. A missing file is reported as missing generated NAME and a different one as not matching, each by name. Any discrepancy produces an error message and a non-zero exit code.

tools/check invokes this check across all registered applications containing sketch: declarations as part of repository verification (./check.sh).

# Build integration

When compiling an application target that declares sketch:,
tools/build automatically checks whether main.cpp is absent or
older than the application manifest or any declared .ino sketch
file. If main.cpp is missing or out of date, the build invokes
sketch on the application directory before proceeding with
compilation. If file: is omitted in a sketch application's
manifest, or if an explicit file: list contains helper sources
without main.cpp, the build ensures main.cpp is included in
compilation.

An application that also declares sketch-library: receives a
second, separate check: the build runs sketch --check silently;
if it fails, it runs sketch to repair and then runs sketch --check
again with stderr visible. If the second check fails, the build
fails with that check's diagnostic, which is always the current
truth. Nothing from the first check is captured or printed.

The using-declarations in the header are required. \<cstdint\>
guarantees the fixed-width integer names in namespace std and
leaves their presence in the global namespace unspecified. A
vendored library writing unqualified uint8_t relies on a compiler
rather than on the language without them. The declared set is
the eight fixed-width types, size_t, and ptrdiff_t.

# Library sketches

A library's example sketches are the ordinary reason a sketch includes a header
it does not own. The library is the tree and the examples are applications
within it, so the library root carries project: and the examples are reached
through folder: entries from it:

Opening fence
AnalogPin/
  mm.mdy                      kind: dir, project: ../../../modules.cpp,
                              folder: examples
  AnalogPin.h                 the library interface
  AnalogPin.cpp               the library implementation
  examples/mm.mdy             kind: dir, folder: AnalogPin
  examples/AnalogPin/mm.mdy   kind: app, sketch: AnalogPin.ino,
                              sketch-library: ../..
  examples/AnalogPin/main.cpp generated, committed beside the sketch
Closing fence

sketch-library names the library root relative to the application manifest. It
requires sketch: on the same manifest, must resolve to an existing directory
inside the owning tree, and may climb to reach it (further entries, for other
libraries, follow the rule in Other libraries an example uses below); docs/modules-mm.mdy owns the
key and states why climbing is permitted here.

The declaration contributes two things to the application, and nothing to any
other target:

1. One include directory, so the quoted include in the sketch resolves. The two
   sketch library layouts are distinguished by a src/ directory: when one
   exists it is the include directory, and otherwise the library root is.
2. Every C and C++ source of the library, compiled into the application and
   linked with it. A layered library contributes its sources recursively from
   src/; a flat one contributes the sources directly in the root, which is what
   keeps examples/ and test/ out of the compiler. Sources are sorted, because
   directory order is not defined and link order is.

Library objects are placed under the application's own output prefix, so two
examples of one library each compile the library they were built against rather
than sharing an object.

## Other libraries an example uses

An example may include a header from a library other than the one it
exercises, and a library may need another to compile at all: Adafruit's GFX
library includes Adafruit BusIO's headers from its own. The Arduino tools find
such a library by the header's name among every library installed; a
modules.cpp application instead declares every input, so the sketch tool finds
the libraries once, in library mode, and records each as a further
sketch-library: entry after the first:

Opening fence
sketch-library: ../..
sketch-library: ../../../Adafruit_ILI9341
sketch-library: ../../../Adafruit_BusIO
Closing fence

Each further entry contributes an include directory and its sources exactly as
the first does, after it. It may lie outside the owning tree, where libraries
are installed, but must hold a library.properties or library.json, so a stray
path compiles nothing; its objects are placed under the application's prefix
in sketch-libraries/NAME/, so two libraries with a source of the same name do
not collide.

The search path is the folder holding the library, whose other libraries were
installed beside it and so come first, then each folder MM_SKETCH_LIBRARIES_PATH
names, separated by colons as PATH is. A folder holds libraries as
subdirectories; an entry that is itself a sketch library stands for that
library alone. A library is a directory holding a library.properties or
library.json; its name is library.properties' name=, else the directory's, and
its include directory is src/ when it has one.

Two things choose a library:

1. The example's own files: its sketches and any header or source beside them,
   generated files excepted. Each header one of them includes, and that neither
   the example, a library already chosen, nor the compatibility headers
   provide, is looked up in the search path: the library whose include
   directory holds it, one named after the header's stem first, and otherwise
   the first in search order. A header nothing provides is left alone, since
   it may be the compiler's.
2. depends= in library.properties, the exercised library's first and then each
   chosen library's, transitively. A name matches a library's name= or its
   directory, spaces read as underscores or hyphens, ignoring case; a version
   constraint after the name is ignored. A name nothing in the search path
   declares is reported as a warning naming the folders searched, and the
   example is generated without it.

A library's own sources are never scanned for includes. They choose a header
by preprocessor conditions the sketch tool does not evaluate, and a header a
library includes only for another board would otherwise bring in whatever
library happens to provide it. depends= is how a library says what it needs.

The sketch folder's own C and C++ sources, other than main.cpp and anything
sketch generated, are compiled with it, as the Arduino tools compile every
source in a sketch folder: library mode writes each as a further file: entry
after main.cpp (Adafruit GFX's GFXcanvas example ships
GFXcanvasSerialDemo.cpp beside its sketch).

The manifests are the record. A later run adds any library or source it finds
that a manifest lacks, after its last sketch-library: line, and removes nothing;
sketch --check and the build's regeneration read the entries back and search
nothing, so a build does not depend on the variable. To drop a library, delete
its line.

# The synthesised compatibility header

A vendored sketch library includes a compatibility header unconditionally,
because that is what its own toolchain provides, and it includes it by the name
that toolchain uses. This project calls its own Sketch.h and generates the
foreign spelling beside it as a forwarder, described under the headers a
library includes by other names below: no rename reaches inside a checkout, so
the spelling survives there and in no other part of this project. modules.cpp
has no such header and no intention of acquiring one as a project asset, so
the sketch tool synthesises
it the same way it synthesises main.cpp: written into the application
directory, committed beside the sketch, regenerated when stale, and compared
byte-for-byte under sketch --check. Every sketch application receives it,
whether or not it declares sketch-library, because main.cpp includes it: the
sketch text itself relies on the same spellings the library does, and a
standalone sketch has no library header to reach them through.

The header is generated rather than shipped for the same reason main.cpp is.
Both are the project's answer to a foreign convention, both belong to the
application that needs them, and neither is code anyone is expected to read
twice. Generating it also keeps the application directory self-describing: the
header names its own generator, and a reader of that directory sees the whole
translation, not a reference to a header living somewhere in the toolchain.

Its contents are fixed by this release and come entirely from mm.sketch:

Opening fence
#pragma once
#include <cstddef>
#include <cstdint>
import mm.sketch;
using namespace mm::sketch;
using std::int8_t;    using std::uint8_t;
using std::int16_t;   using std::uint16_t;
using std::int32_t;   using std::uint32_t;
using std::int64_t;   using std::uint64_t;
using std::size_t;    using std::ptrdiff_t;
#define F(string_literal) (string_literal)
#define PSTR(string_literal) (string_literal)
#define PROGMEM
using std::malloc;  // and calloc, realloc, free
using std::memcpy;  // and the other <cstring> routines
inline std::uint8_t pgm_read_byte(const void* address);  // and the rest
#define NAN, #define INFINITY
#define word(...) makeWord(__VA_ARGS__)
inline constexpr unsigned int A0 = 0;  // through A7
inline constexpr double PI = 3.1415926535897932384626433832795;
inline constexpr double HALF_PI, TWO_PI, EULER, DEG_TO_RAD, RAD_TO_DEG
inline void yield() { dispatch(); }
inline constexpr unsigned int B0 = 0;  // through B11111111
Closing fence

Three of those lines are spellings this specification and
docs/modules-sketch.mdy decline as behaviour. Declining the behaviour and
declining the spelling are different decisions: F and PROGMEM exist so a sketch
can place a constant in flash, which is the linker's business here, so the
macros are accepted and inert and the argument is its own value. A library that
writes F("x") compiles; nothing is placed anywhere.

The analog channel names number the channels in declaration order. A board's
real mapping belongs to mm.mcu, so a sketch naming a channel its board does not
have fails when it reads that channel, latching Status::Unsupported, rather
than when it names it.

The mathematical constants are the values docs/modules-sketch.mdy's Math and
trigonometry section takes but does not name: it exports sin, cos, tan and
pow through using declarations, and a sketch that writes sin(angle * PI / 180)
needs the constant as well as the function. They are declared at the precision
of a double, which is the precision every one of those functions works in.

yield is the foreign spelling of dispatch, which docs/modules-sketch.mdy
describes under Dispatch and latency: the point at which a sketch waiting in a
loop of its own lets interrupt handlers and the serial callback run. The
behaviour is not declined here, only the name, so the header forwards one to
the other rather than defining anything.

word is the one spelling here that has to be a macro rather than a constant or
a function. mm.sketch exports both a type named word and a function named
makeWord, and a sketch writes word(h, l) for the second and word x for the
first. A function-like macro expands only where a call follows, which is what
lets one name do both, and is why upstream defines it the same way.

The C allocator and the string routines are there because a sketch library
reaches them through the compatibility header rather than by naming
<stdlib.h> or <string.h> itself, which is the same reason the integer types
are there.

The flash readers are the other half of PROGMEM. Nothing was placed in a
separate address space, so pgm_read_byte reads the object it is given the
address of, and _near and _far are the same read. memcpy_P and its neighbours
are the same statement about the string routines.

The bit patterns are AVR's binary.h, which a sketch reaches through the
compatibility header.
Every pattern from one to eight digits is a separate name there, so every one
of them is a separate name here, and there are five hundred and ten of them.
They are constants rather than macros for the reason constrain and map are
functions rather than macros: a name with a type and a scope is the one this
project can reason about, and nothing about a bit pattern needs the
preprocessor.

Because the header is generated and verified byte-for-byte by the sketch
tool for sketch applications (whether external or registered in-tree), it is
not authored project code: the preprocessor directives in it are the ones a
foreign library requires, and docs/modules-c++20.mdy's prohibition governs what
this project authors for itself.

## The headers a library includes by other names

A sketch toolchain ships its compatibility header under its own name, and
ships Print.h, Printable.h, Wire.h and SPI.h as separate files beside it. A
vendored library includes whichever it needs by that name. Each of those names
something mm.sketch already exports, so the sketch tool writes each as a
one-line header beside Sketch.h that includes it. A library reaching for
Print.h finds Print; nothing is declared twice, and the compatibility surface
stays in one file.

The foreign name of the compatibility header itself is generated the same way
and for the same reason. It is a forwarder like the others, and it is the only
place in this project that spells it: a vendored source writes that include and
no rename reaches inside a checkout, so the spelling belongs in a generated
file rather than in the project's own vocabulary.

A name with nothing behind it is not generated. Stream.h is the case that makes
the rule worth stating: mm.sketch exports no Stream, so a header that resolved
and then failed to declare one would turn a missing-file diagnostic naming the
library's dependency into a wall of missing-class errors inside it.

These are generated, committed, regenerated and checked exactly as Sketch.h
is, and sketch --check reports each by name.

The build puts the application directory on the include path ahead of the
library directories, which is what lets a library header two directories away
resolve the compatibility header against the application that is compiling
it. Every sketch application receives that entry, a standalone one included,
so a sketch that spells the include itself reaches the same file rather than
a second one.

# The legacy profile

sketch --legacy DIRECTORY, and sketch --library --legacy LIBRARY for every
example of a library, generate applications in mm.sketch's legacy profile. The
manifest gains sketch-profile: legacy, written into a new manifest or added to
an existing one's front matter, and the generated files differ from the core
profile's in two lines each: main.cpp's header reads (mm: 1.3, legacy profile)
and its prelude adds using namespace mm::sketch::legacy after using namespace
mm::sketch, and Sketch.h does the same for the library sources that include
it. The five forwarders are unchanged.

A legacy main.cpp also includes Sketch.h first, ahead of the hoisted includes,
where a sketch toolchain puts Arduino.h. A library header that includes nothing
itself and relies on the sketch toolchain having declared uint32_t, Serial, or
sqrt before it, as RobTillaart's Kurtosis.h does, compiles only in that order.
The core profile keeps Sketch.h last, for the reason Prelude and hoisted
includes gives; in the legacy profile a standard header the sketch includes
comes after the import, the arrangement the core profile avoids, which is the
same arrangement a library header reaches when it includes Arduino.h before a
standard header of its own.

The build compiles a legacy application with ARDUINO defined as 10819, the
value the Arduino IDE 1.8.19 passes, on the command line of every file it
compiles for it: main.cpp and the sources of its sketch libraries alike, since
a library's own source tests ARDUINO before it includes Arduino.h. A library
that chooses its platform by that macro, as RF24 does, otherwise takes a
branch written for a native toolchain rather than the Arduino API. No board or
architecture macro, ARDUINO_ARCH_RP2040 or ARDUINO_AVR_UNO for instance, is
defined: those select board-specific code, which is what mm.sketch replaces. A
core application is compiled with no definition.

With ARDUINO defined and no architecture named, a library written for the
Arduino cores often takes this host for an AVR, since it knows no other
Arduino without __arm__. The legacy profile therefore also supplies what an
AVR core's Arduino.h brings in and an Arduino core for another architecture
ships for AVR code:

- four more forwarders, written, checked, and committed like the other five:
  avr/pgmspace.h, one directory down, whose declarations Sketch.h already has;
  pins_arduino.h and wiring_private.h, which every Arduino core ships and
  display libraries include unconditionally; and SD.h, Arduino's SD library,
  redirected to SdFat, which serves its calls better: the forwarder includes
  SdFat.h and defines SD as an SdFat, and library mode adds the SdFat library
  to any example whose own files include SD.h;
- an include inside #if, #ifdef, or #ifndef stays where it is in main.cpp
  rather than being hoisted, as a sketch toolchain leaves it, so a sketch that
  chooses a header by a condition (SdFat's USE_SD_H) includes only the one it
  chose;
- in Sketch.h, <cctype> and its character routines, toupper among them;
  _BV(bit); SPI_HAS_TRANSACTION, the promise SPI.h makes that SPISettings and
  beginTransaction exist, without which a library falls back to
  setClockDivider and the calls it replaced; and printf_P, sprintf_P, and
  snprintf_P, each its counterpart without _P, given the format with AVR's %S,
  a string in flash, read as %s, since nothing is placed in flash here;
- in Sketch.h, SPI.h's integer modes: SPI_MODE0 to SPI_MODE3 are 0 to 3, and
  SPISettings names mm::sketch::legacy::SPISettings, the core class with one
  more constructor taking the mode as an integer, so a library that keeps a
  mode in a uint8_t and hands it back compiles (Adafruit BusIO does). Both
  are macros, as in SPI.h, so mm::sketch::SPISettings spelled out in a legacy
  sketch is rewritten too; write SPISettings;
- in Sketch.h, radians and degrees, and a declaration of __FlashStringHelper,
  the type of an F() string, which a library names in overloads of its own;
- the flash readers' names parenthesised where Sketch.h defines them, so a
  library that defined pgm_read_byte as a macro before including Arduino.h,
  as Adafruit_SSD1306 does off AVR, keeps its macro and the definition
  stands.

RF24 builds its current examples on the host and the Pico with these, in
the legacy profile, and Adafruit GFX builds its examples on the host.

The manifest, not the command line, is the record. A later run without
--legacy regenerates a legacy application as legacy, the build's regeneration
does the same, and --check compares each application with the profile its
manifest selects. --check --legacy additionally refuses an application whose
manifest does not select it. To return an application to the core profile,
remove the key and rerun sketch.

# Library mode

The sketch tool provides library mode for discovering, manifest-generating,
and validating an entire vendored sketch library tree containing example
sketches.

## Invocation and metadata detection

Library mode is requested explicitly via the `--library` CLI option or
detected automatically. The tool recognises a sketch library root when the
target directory contains an `examples` subdirectory alongside either a
`library.properties` or `library.json` metadata file (both regular files).
Passing `--library` enforces library processing even if metadata files are
absent. When the library tree is located outside any enclosing project, the
tool determines the project root via `--project DIR` or by locating the
installation containing the sketch tool itself.

## Recursive discovery rules

Discovery explores only the `examples/` subtree of the library:
- Dotfiles and hidden directories (names starting with `.`) are ignored.
- Non-example subdirectories such as `src/` and `test/` are never entered.
- Symbolic links are skipped to prevent recursion cycles and link escapes.

The walk distinguishes application, category, and leaf directories:
- Direct sketch container: any subdirectory holding one or more `.ino` sketch
  files is an application. Sketches directly under `examples/` are forbidden;
  each sketch must reside in its own subdirectory.
- Category directory: any directory holding only subdirectories is an
  intermediate category node.
- Empty or non-sketch leaf: a directory containing no sketches and no child
  subdirectories is skipped without generating a manifest.
- Ambiguous sketch container: if a directory contains multiple `.ino` files
  but none matches `<dirname>.ino`, the directory is skipped as ambiguous.
- Own main.cpp: an application directory that already holds a main.cpp whose
  first line is not the header sketch writes is the example's own source,
  which another toolchain compiles beside the .ino (RF24's pingpair_maple
  ships one for the Maple IDE). The tool never overwrites it: library mode
  reports the example as skipped and writes nothing in it, and a single
  application is refused with exit 65.

If the walk yields no valid applications, library mode fails and exits 65
without writing manifests.

## Sketch ordering and application naming

Within each application directory:
- The primary sketch is `<dirname>.ino` if present, or the single `.ino` file
  when only one exists.
- Additional `.ino` files are sorted alphabetically and appended after the
  primary sketch.

Application names are derived from their relative path from `examples/`, with
path separators (`/`) replaced by hyphens (`-`).
If two paths derive the same application name, library mode refuses the tree.

## Manifest generation and layout

Library mode generates manifests for the root, intermediate categories, and
each discovered application:
- Library root (`mm.mdy`): declares `mm: 1.3`, `kind: dir`,
  `name: <directory-name>`, `folder: examples`, and an optional
  `project: <path>` resolving to the enclosing project root.
- Intermediate and examples nodes (`mm.mdy`): declare `mm: 1.3`, `kind: dir`,
  `name: <directory-name>`, and sorted `folder:` entries naming child
  subdirectories.
- Application nodes (`mm.mdy`): declare `mm: 1.3`, `kind: app`,
  `name: <app-name>`, `use: mm.sketch`, `file: main.cpp`, ordered `sketch:`
  entries, and `sketch-library: <climb-to-root>`.

All identifiers, directory names, and filenames undergo scalar validation
to reject empty strings, newlines, and illegal characters.

## Compatibility and manifest preservation

Existing manifests are preserved byte-for-byte when compatible:
- Root manifest: must have `kind: dir`, matching `name:`, must match `project:`
  expectations (resolving to the same canonical project root), and must have
  an exact match of `folder:` entries without duplicates.
- Category manifest: must have `kind: dir`, matching `name:`, and an exact
  match of `folder:` entries without duplicates.
- Application manifest: must have `kind: app`, matching `name:`,
  `use: mm.sketch`, `file: main.cpp`, exact match of `sketch:` entries in
  declared order, and a matching `sketch-library:` relative path.

Any incompatibility causes the sketch tool to report the discrepancies and
exit 65 without modifying the filesystem.

## Integrity checking (--check)

When invoked with `--check` on a library tree, the tool validates every
manifest (`mm.mdy`) semantically against the discovered tree. Generated
sources (`main.cpp`) and compatibility headers (`Sketch.h` and its
forwarders) are compared
byte-for-byte with their expected contents in memory. All validation and file
mismatches across the entire tree are collected and reported before the tool
exits with status 65.

# Source guard

All generated files (main.cpp) and manifests (mm.mdy) are written using
atomic, guarded filesystem operations provided by mm::ino::write_guarded:
- Pre-existing files and symlinks: an existing destination is replaced
  atomically via POSIX renameat rather than overwritten through in place. A
  pre-planted symlink is replaced by the generated file, leaving the symlink's
  target untouched.
- Exclusive temporary creation: temporary files are opened exclusively using
  openat with O_CREAT | O_EXCL | O_NOFOLLOW | O_WRONLY. If the temporary name
  already exists, link or file, the creation fails immediately.
- Failure cleanup: write, close, or rename errors invoke unlinkat on the
  temporary file and leave the destination intact.
- Path safety: sketch: entries must be relative non-climbing paths within the
  application directory. Absolute paths, climbing .. segments, and symlinks
  resolving outside the directory are rejected.
